// over-the-wall-on-a-box (styled worlds A): an autumn valley built as one table automaton of hand-painted enamelled
// tin over brass works. Clockwork foxes and hares play hide and seek round a walled orchard: the hares have shut its
// gates with apple crates, and the foxes have found out how to ride a crate over the wall.
// THE SECOND VERSION (the owner's round on the designer's lines): from the first version's high arrival the flight now comes down onto the river belt and goes up it low,
// over the bridge and beside the poplars, to the geese; from route metre 1,130 on the line is the first version's but for a sooner rise over the orchard's north wall.
// The way's length is the first version's to within 0.6 m at every typed point from metre 1,130 on. The light is deeper: less fill and haze, a little more sun.
//
// THE MAP (typed, the same on every visit): north is +z, west is +x. A tin tray on a wooden bench. Two ranges of
// painted hill flats in five rows each stand along its east and west sides, every flat turned 42 degrees toward the
// south like the wings of a toy theatre, and three rows straight across (150, 215 and 290 m) close the valley's head. Between them:
// the river belt, the foxes' counting yard, the walled orchard, the clouds on rods, the works, the crate yard and the
// one great crate (150 m a side) whose south face is the way out. Beyond the tray, standing on the bench itself and
// mountain-high: an oil can, a paint tin with its brush laid across and a pencil leant on it, a cotton reel.
//
// WHAT MOVES, AND BY WHAT.
// BY THE PLACE'S OWN CLOCK (the engine's time for this world), the same with no visitor and from the first metre:
//   the hill flats rise and fall through their slots on brass posts in a slow wave that runs up the valley (once round in 14 s); the
//   poplars, the apple trees, the boats, the geese on their rods and the clouds on theirs ride cams in the same way;
//   the geese beat their wings; the river's steel runs; every toy's key turns in its back; the great wheels, the
//   mainspring's drum, the governor, the winding key and a windmill's sails turn; hares rise on lifts behind the wall and behind crates
//   until their ears show, and sink again (behind each of the eight crates of the avenue to the way out a hare sits up so, never wholly hid). Three more rise so outside the orchard's north wall and look in over it.
//   STANDING TOYS, their keys turning: the counting fox at its post, two foxes that wait on their crates, a hare crouched round the practice
//   wall's post and one sitting up behind the wall; before the works a fox upright on a crate and two hares crouched behind two crates; a fox
//   on its crate in the avenue; the hares that hide in the orchard and the crate yard.
//   THE RIDES OF THE CLOCK: ten foxes, each upright on an apple crate, go round two circuits (five on each): up in
//   the yard kennel, up a ramp and over the yard's practice wall, along a slot in the tray to a ramp at the orchard's south wall, up the ramp, OVER THE WALL in a
//   flight of 2.6 s, down the landing ramp inside, along the lane to the fox door, down, and back under the tray.
//   The two circuits are half a turn out of step at the kennel: a rider comes up every 5.6 s, now for one ramp, now for the other
//   (at the orchard wall the two arrive 1.6 s apart, then 9.6 s pass). COUNTED (tools/sa/otw-count.mjs rides 40): on 40 of 40 deals a visitor has one whole ride of the
//   clock in the picture, from 100 m before a ramp's foot to the landing ramp's top (the tool's head says how it
//   counts). THAT COUNT IS OF THE FIRST VERSION and was not run again for the second (the tool stands on its maker's machine): it holds because from route
//   metre 1,320 to 2,690 (the yard, the practice wall, the south wall, the flight over it and the landing ramp) the flown line is the first version's to within
//   0.1 m and the looks there are untouched. The seed deals only where in their round the circuits start.
// BY PLACE (the metres of route flown), ONE THING, AND IT IS THE WORLD'S ONE ANSWER TO THE VISITOR: the fox that
//   waits on its crate before the third ramp (the westmost, beside the shut south gate) goes when we come along the
//   wall's front toward it (metre SB0 of the route) and from there moves 0.9 m for every metre we fly: up its ramp
//   and over the wall to our left a little ahead of us, and it stands on the landing ramp inside as we cross the wall
//   ourselves. With the eye still it stands still. Nothing else answers the visitor.
// THE LOOKS (pois) and the pace are by the metres flown; the look at the answering fox follows the fox.
// Nothing here is begun, ended, moved or lit by a typed time, a typed share of the flight, an inherited key or a seed.
// The seed deals outlines, paints, places of field trees and clouds, and the starts of cams and circuits (streams of
// this world's own, one for each kind). THE VISIT (ctx.vary.density) deals how closely the apple trees and the
// fields' trees stand; a small machine (ctx.quality.detail) gets fewer field trees and haystacks.
//
// A BODY AND THE LENS: no apple tree stands within 22 m of the flown line in plan, no field tree or haystack
// within 36 m, no cloud within 110 m at its own height and no cloud's rod within 110 m in plan at any height; a term of sight, named here: no cloud stands in plan within its own half width and 30 m of a
// straight line to the sun's arm from any point of the flown line between route metres 2950 and 3850 that lies 60 m and more south of the sun (the clipped
// cones, 17 m high, line the riders' straights, where the line is 54 m up and more); the apple baskets stand 10 m and more to its side and are 7 m high where the
// line is 12 m up and more; the nearest goose is 29 m off it in plan; the hare that hides beside the line in the orchard crouches 26 m from it; the riders' slots pass under the line only
// where it is 74 m up and more (a rider's ears reach 43 m); the practice wall's east post is 80 m west of the line, its ramps and the riders on them 120 m and more; the answering fox flies 40 m to our left; the hill
// flats' nearest ends are 40 m and more east of the line where it runs up the valley beside them. The river belt's two end rollers lie with their tops under 5 m (the line
// passes 41 m over the north one); up the river the line is 14 m and more over the bridge's rails and 30 m and more from the poplars' leaves; the three hares outside the
// north wall stand 65 m and more to the side of where the line crosses it, 58 m up.
// SHADOWS: one sun, every body's shadow laid on the tray along it; the two great wheels throw the shadow of their
// rims without the teeth (a toothed shadow drawn out along the low sun read as broken steps).
//
// WHAT READS THE EYE OR THE PIXEL (section 153) is named at the head of the look, src/parts/over-the-wall-on-a-box.js.
// THE BUILD'S STEPS: every long loop yields. Triangles are counted in marks.tris (heaviest of seeds 1 to 200 at the
// fullest density: 226,468 by this world's own count, tools/sa/otw-count.mjs tris, for the first version; the second adds three hares and the kennels' roofs, bands
// and doors, 7,016 triangles on seed 7 by the gate's count (219,923 to 226,939), and was not counted again over the 200 seeds).
// PREVIEWS with strangers are used in the making (section 172).
